<section id="story" class="wp-block-group story"><!-- wp:group {"className":"story-pin"} -->
<div class="wp-block-group story-pin"><!-- wp:group {"className":"story-l"} -->
<div class="wp-block-group story-l"><!-- wp:html -->
<span class="lab kick">How it works</span>
<!-- /wp:html -->
<!-- wp:heading {"className":"d3"} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading d3">Gain in centimetres, <em>not metres.</em></h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:html -->
<div aria-hidden="true" class="rail"><i class="prog" id="rprog"></i><span class="on">Silica</span><span>Phosphate</span><span>Cavity</span></div>
<!-- /wp:html -->
<!-- wp:group {"className":"steps"} -->
<div class="wp-block-group steps"><!-- wp:group {"tagName":"article","className":"step on"} -->
<article class="wp-block-group step on"><!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Silica runs out of room.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Most active fibers use silica glass, but its rare-earth dopants (erbium, ytterbium, thulium, holmium) cluster at high concentrations, which degrades gain.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></article>
<!-- /wp:group -->
<!-- wp:group {"tagName":"article","className":"step"} -->
<article class="wp-block-group step"><!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Our phosphate glass doesn’t.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Our phosphate fibers hold dopant concentrations up to 100 times those of silica with no loss of optical gain, typically 5 dB of gain per centimetre.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></article>
<!-- /wp:group -->
<!-- wp:group {"tagName":"article","className":"step"} -->
<article class="wp-block-group step"><!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">A laser under 2 cm long.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A fiber cavity shorter than 2 cm gives single-frequency lasers with linewidths below 500 Hz and low phase noise, in a compact, robust package.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></article>
<!-- /wp:group --></div>
<!-- /wp:group --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->
